WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Every time this egg divides, it destroys one of its own proteins.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
Then it builds it up again.

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.600
Why would a cell destroy its engine's partner, every single time?

4
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:20.590
In 1982, at Woods Hole, Tim Hunt's group saw this in dividing sea-urchin eggs.

5
00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:25.710
The protein climbed through each cycle and vanished at every division.

6
00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.130
They named it cyclin.

7
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:30.770
The eggs make it all the time.

8
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.650
It falls because it is destroyed.

9
00:00:35.583 --> 00:00:40.663
Cyclin's partner is a kinase, a CDK, or cyclin-dependent kinase.

10
00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:43.743
Alone, the CDK is inactive.

11
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:47.463
A bound cyclin is needed to switch it on.

12
00:00:48.723 --> 00:00:53.223
Every eukaryotic cell, from yeast to human, runs its cycle on this engine.

13
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:57.527
Now add a second trace: the kinase's activity.

14
00:00:58.807 --> 00:01:02.847
In cycles like a frog embryo's first, it does not follow the ramp.

15
00:01:04.087 --> 00:01:06.087
It stays low through most of interphase.

16
00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:12.067
Then it can snap up just before mitosis, and crash as cyclin is destroyed.

17
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.387
Here is the puzzle.

18
00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:19.027
Cyclin climbs steadily, and cyclin switches the kinase on.

19
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.547
So why does the activity wait, and then jump?

20
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.990
Because a pair can be built and still be held off.

21
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:40.470
The mitotic pair is CDK1 with cyclin B. All through G2, Wee1 adds an inhibitory

22
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.970
phosphate to each new pair.

23
00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.650
Inactive pairs pile up, loaded and waiting.

24
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.910
Then a phosphatase, Cdc25, takes the phosphate off.

25
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:57.650
Once a little CDK1 escapes, it boosts Cdc25 and slows Wee1.

26
00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.510
Each active pair frees more.

27
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.570
Activity does not creep; it snaps, all or none.

28
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.410
Checkpoints pull the same levers.

29
00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:15.250
If DNA is damaged or unfinished in G2, sensor kinases switch off Cdc25.

30
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.350
CDK1 keeps its brake, and the cell waits.

31
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:21.970
Here is the one idea.

32
00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.470
CDK activity rises and falls once per cycle.

33
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.750
The rise carries the cell from G1, through S, into mitosis.

34
00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.970
Destroying cyclin brings the fall.

35
00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.050
And that one rise and fall copies the genome once.

36
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:46.893
Now the fall. It starts with one enzyme: the APC/C, or anaphase-promoting complex.

37
00:02:48.273 --> 00:02:52.913
Until every chromosome grips the spindle, a checkpoint holds the APC/C back.

38
00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:55.773
The cell waits before anaphase.

39
00:02:56.833 --> 00:03:01.893
Once the checkpoint falls silent, the APC/C tags two proteins for destruction.

40
00:03:03.393 --> 00:03:05.973
Securin goes, so the sister chromatids separate.

41
00:03:07.493 --> 00:03:11.193
Cyclin B goes, so activity falls and the cell leaves mitosis.

42
00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:14.287
Why not just switch the kinase off?

43
00:03:15.507 --> 00:03:18.327
A phosphate can be removed; that is only a pause.

44
00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:22.307
A destroyed protein cannot be switched back on.

45
00:03:23.407 --> 00:03:24.647
It must be made again.

46
00:03:25.787 --> 00:03:28.967
That is a main reason the big transitions run one way.

47
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:34.570
Here is the test. Frog egg extracts cycle in a tube.

48
00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:37.930
Give them a cyclin that cannot be destroyed.

49
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.250
What happens?

50
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.500
The extract went into mitosis.

51
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.220
The kinase stayed active, and the extract did not come out.

52
00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.040
Making cyclin drives entry.

53
00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.740
Destroying it permits exit.

54
00:03:59.783 --> 00:04:02.283
The same rise and fall controls copying.

55
00:04:03.583 --> 00:04:07.483
An origin of replication must be licensed before it can fire.

56
00:04:08.923 --> 00:04:13.923
Licensing happens only while activity is low, in late mitosis and G one.

57
00:04:15.263 --> 00:04:17.763
Firing needs the rising activity of S phase.

58
00:04:18.917 --> 00:04:24.257
High activity also blocks new licences, in yeast, and helps block them in animal

59
00:04:24.257 --> 00:04:28.777
cells. So each origin can fire at most once per cycle.

60
00:04:30.097 --> 00:04:33.277
New licences wait for the fall, normally at mitosis.

61
00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:36.367
Fission-yeast cells test the rule.

62
00:04:37.607 --> 00:04:42.687
Without their mitotic cyclin, activity falls after S phase, with no mitosis.

63
00:04:44.487 --> 00:04:49.367
Origins are licensed afresh, and the cells copy their DNA again, without dividing.

64
00:04:51.117 --> 00:04:53.337
So why destroy cyclin every time?

65
00:04:54.557 --> 00:04:57.997
Because the kinase must climb once and fall once per cycle.

66
00:04:59.377 --> 00:05:02.257
Destruction is what normally makes the fall one way.

67
00:05:03.317 --> 00:05:09.057
The cell leaves mitosis, resets its origins, and copies its genome once per cycle.
